      {
        "review_id": 348645602,
        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r348645602-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "Bring Kleenex...Be Prepared to Possibly Shed Tears",
        "text": "I knew they cremated bodies here.\nI knew this place was where the loved ones said goodbye to their deceased.  \nI knew Hindi from all over the world traveled here to worship.\nBut I was not prepared for the emotions that swept over me and took their hold upon me as I saw the left living ones mourning over their deceased loved ones.  Clearly they were not ready to let go - who is when death comes calling - especially unexpectedly?  The sounds of the crying wails ripped into my heart and my own tears came pouring out in great abundance.  \nThis was the 1st time I smelled burning human flesh and saw my 1st cremations.  If you enter with the Hindi pilgrims, you may see multiple vehicles bringing in the dead.  The crowds moved me along and then I felt something hit me on my side.  It was a dangling arm from a skinny naked dead woman with a purple cloth on top of her.  I wondered who she was and where her clothing went.  Did they recycle it?  Did someone steal it?  I was glad that someone threw the purple cloth on top of her to provide her some dignity in death.  There were no mourners following her.  She was alone except for the the cremators and the workers preparing her body to be burned.  I watched her being prepared and stayed with her for part of the burning.  They say an average body takes 3-4 hours to burn.  If it takes longer time than it has a negative attachment I was told.  \nI walked alongside the river and came upon the sadhu's little hut.  I smelled pot wafting thru the air - a pot smoking sadhu taught in the hut I was told.  I did not enter.\nBe sure to check out the old folks home on the premises and the sadhu quarters that are shared with the cows.",
        "review_tip": null,
        "rating": 5,
